hadiths[4]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1107","Narrated Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him): I heard a man reciting a verse, and I had heard it differently from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I took hold of his hand and brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Both of you are reciting correctly, do not differ. Those before you were destroyed only because of their differences."""
"1108","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that one day two men abused each other; one of them was a Muslim and the other was a Jew. During the conversation, the Muslim said, ""By Him Who gave Muhammad (peace be upon him) superiority over all the worlds."" The Jew said, ""By Him Who gave Musa (peace be upon him) superiority over all the worlds."" So the Muslim raised his hand and slapped the Jew on the face. The Jew went to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and related what the Muslim had done. The Prophet (peace be upon him) called for the Muslim and asked him about the incident. He narrated the whole event, so the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Do not give me superiority over Musa (peace be upon him), for on the Day of Resurrection all people will fall unconscious, and I will also fall unconscious with them. I will be the first to regain consciousness, and I will see Musa (peace be upon him) holding one of the legs of the Throne. Now, I do not know whether he also fell unconscious and regained consciousness before me, or whether he was among those whom Allah exempted from unconsciousness."""
"1109","Narrated Anas (may Allah be pleased with him): A Jew crushed the head of a girl between two stones. She was asked, ""Who did this to you? Was it so-and-so? Was it so-and-so?"" until the name of that Jew was mentioned, and she indicated with her head that it was him. So the Jew was caught and he confessed to the crime. Therefore, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered that his head be crushed between two stones as well."
"1110","The hadith of Sayyiduna Ash'ath (may Allah be pleased with him) has already been mentioned earlier (see the Book: Statement regarding Musaqat... Chapter: Disputing over a well and its judgment...). In one narration, it is said that he was a Hadrami, and in another narration, it is mentioned that he was a Jew with whom Sayyiduna Ash'ath (may Allah be pleased with him) had a dispute."
